  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**\n   Our solution addresses the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by combining multi-resolution analysis, state-of-the-art optimization techniques, and scalable mathematical transformations. We maintain two key ideas:\n   - **Explicit symmetry breaking** through dual Legendre transforms\n   - **Adaptive resolution management** guided by error-aware grid promotion\n\n   The limitation addressed here is the excessive numerical cost of high-resolution searches in previous approaches. Our method evaluates candidates at increasing resolutions while maintaining mathematical equivalence through integral transforms.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**\n   The optimization problem is defined as:\n   ```\n   min_C5 max_k \u222b_0^2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   ```\n   subject to:\n   ```\n   \u222b_0^2 h(x) dx = 1\n   0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n   ```\n\n   Our grid transformation is:\n   ```\n   Let V(n) be a valid representation satisfying the constraints\n   Define transformation: V(n) \u2192 V(2n)\n   Such that:\n       \u222b_0^2 V(n)(x) dx = \u222b_0^2 V(2n)(x) dx\n       For each k: \u222b_0^2 V(n)(x)(1-V(n)(x+k)) dx = (dx_n) * \u222b_0^2 V(2n)(x)(1-V(2n)(x+k)) dx\n   ```\n\n   We use integral transforms to maintain equivalence:\n   ```\n   Let T denote the transformation operator maintaining:\n       C5(h) = C5(h_{transformed})\n   ```\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**\n   ```\n   def run(seed=42, budget_s=1100, **kwargs):\n       # Initialization phase\n       h_base = get_parent_or_uniform_solution()  # 400-point representation\n       results = []\n\n       # Phase 1: Symmetry-adapted S-LQP on base grid\n       h_orig, score_orig, n_orig = optimize_base_grid(h_base)\n\n       # Phase 2: Progressive resolution doubling\n       time_remaining = budget_s - time_elapsed()\n       if time_remaining < 30: return h_orig, score_orig, n_orig\n\n       # Transformation module\n       def extend_grid(h_current):\n           # Grid doubling transformation\n           h_doubled = transform_solution(h_current, double=True)\n           # Perform optimization on new grid\n           return optimize_base_grid(h_doubled)\n\n       # Adaptive grid escalation\n       gradiens = []\n       for _ in range(min(3, int(time_remaining/360))):\n           if time_remaining < 60: break\n           try:\n               h_new, score_new, n_new = extend_grid(h_orig)\n               results.append((h_new, score_new))\n               h_orig = h_new\n               n_orig = n_new\n               if score_new < score_orig:\n                   score_orig = score_new\n                   best_solution = h_new\n           except TimeOverrunError:\n               break\n   ```\n\n4. **Search Choices and Useful Variations**\n   ```\n   Optimization Parameters:\n   - Base grid: n_start=400\n   - Smoothmax temperature schedule: \u03b2\u2080=150, \u03b2_max=3500, decay=0.9991\n   - Transformation complexity: exponential scaling with base 1.5\n   - Constrain transformations to meet integral and box conditions\n\n   Alternate Representation:\n   Use piecewise linear interpolation with adaptive weights\n   For integral condition: exact projection ensured by:\n       h_proj[i] = clamp( (h_base[i] * 2) if random() > uniform(0.3,0.7) else h_base[i], 0, 1)\n   ```\n\n5. **Compute Budget and Robustness**\n   ```\n   Phase allocation:\n   - Initial optimization: 70% of time for base resolution\n   - Grid doubling: each doubling costs 1/3 of previous time\n   - Total maximum grid doublings: 3\n   - Minimum grid resolution: 400 points\n\n   Time management:\n   Set time limit per optimization: \n       t(parent_grid) = budget_s\n       t(each doubling) = t(parent_grid)/2.5\n\n   Constraint enforcement:\n   After each transformation:\n       normalize_integral(h)\n       clip_bounds(h)\n       enforce_unit_integral(h)\n\n   Fallback mechanism:\n   - If optimization exceeds time: return last validated solution\n   - If grid transformation fails: return solution with target C5\n   ```\n\n6. **Validation and Return Contract**\n   ```\n   # Post-processing checks\n   def validate_solution(h, target_C5):\n       # Integral validation\n       integral_val = np.sum(h) / (n_points/2)\n       assert abs(integral_val - 1) < 1e-4\n\n       # Box validation\n       if not (np.min(h) >= -1e-6 and np.max(h) <= 1+1e-6):\n           return False\n\n       # C5 re-evaluation\n       corr_val = np.max(np.correlate(h,1-h,mode=\"full\"))\n       actual_C5 = corr_val * (2.0 / n_points)\n       return abs(actual_C5 - target_C5) < 1e-4 or actual_C5 > target_C5\n   ```\n\n   Solution metrics are evaluated according to:\n   ```\n   C5 = max_k \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n   ```\n\n   The solver contracts require all solutions to satisfy:\n   - Normalization: \u222b\u2080\u00b9 h(x) dx = 1\n   - Boundedness: 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1\n   - target_C5 \u2264 0.38085\n\n   All transformations maintain these properties exactly.",
